      "hints": [
        "Equate $2x^2 - 3x + 1 = kx + k^2 \\implies 2x^2 - (3 + k)x + (1 - k^2) = 0$.",
        "Show discriminant $\\Delta = (3k + 1)^2 \\ge 0$ for all $k$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Equate the curve and straight line equations:\n$$2x^2 - 3x + 1 = kx + k^2 \\implies 2x^2 - (3 + k)x + (1 - k^2) = 0$$",
        "Compute the discriminant $\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$ of the resulting quadratic:\n$$\\Delta = [-(3 + k)]^2 - 4(2)(1 - k^2) = (9 + 6k + k^2) - 8(1 - k^2)$$\n$$= 9 + 6k + k^2 - 8 + 8k^2 = 9k^2 + 6k + 1$$",
        "Factorise as a perfect square:\n$$\\Delta = (3k + 1)^2$$\nSince the square of any real number is non-negative, $\\Delta = (3k + 1)^2 \\ge 0$ for all real values of $k$.\nTherefore, the quadratic has at least one real root for every $k$, so the curve and line always meet."
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Discriminant Proof": "Forms quadratic $2x^2 - (3+k)x + 1 - k^2 = 0$ (M1, A1). Expands discriminant to $9k^2 + 6k + 1 = (3k+1)^2$ (A1). Concludes $\\Delta \\ge 0$ (A1)."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
